{"qid":"Q180568","names":["New Kingdom of Egypt"],"title":"New Kingdom of Egypt","endonym":null,"capsule":"The New Kingdom (c. 1550–1069 BC) was ancient Egypt's most expansive imperial phase, spanning the 18th, 19th, and 20th dynasties. Ahmose I expelled the Hyksos and reunified Egypt around 1550 BC, and under Thutmose III (r. 1479–1425 BC) Egyptian armies campaigned repeatedly into the Levant and reached the Euphrates, forging an empire that stretched from Nubia to Syria. Ramesses II (r. 1279–1213 BC) contested Hittite power at Kadesh (1274 BC) and concluded one of history's earliest surviving peace treaties. After Ramesses III (r. 1184–1153 BC) repelled the Sea Peoples, central authority eroded; by about 1069 BC the state fragmented, ushering in the Third Intermediate Period.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. New Kingdom territorial extent in the Levant and Nubia is well-documented by royal inscriptions (Karnak annals), administrative papyri, and the Amarna Letters diplomatic archive; precise frontier lines in the Levantine buffer zones between Egypt and Hatti are somewhat negotiated from textual evidence.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[],"britannica":"topic/New-Kingdom-Egyptian-history","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"New Kingdom of Egypt","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"Ahmose I","nativeName":"𓇳𓏏𓀭 𓇓𓏏𓈖𓆭𓄿𓋴","roman":"Ahmose","start":-1550,"end":-1525,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":null,"name":"Amenhotep I","nativeName":"𓇳𓏏𓀭 𓇓𓏏𓈖𓇋𓏠𓈖𓀭","roman":"Amenhotep","start":-1525,"end":-1504,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":null,"name":"Thutmose I","nativeName":"𓇳𓏏𓀭 𓇓𓏏𓈖𓇋𓏠𓈖𓀭","ro